            "heading": "Sleeping more was never going to fix it — and being told to is the most useless advice in the category",
            "body": "<p>Every man with this problem has done the experiment. Eight hours a night, consistently, for weeks. It helps the morning puffiness a bit. It does nothing about the shadow or the texture underneath, and by day ten you work out that “get more sleep” was not a diagnosis — it was a way of ending the conversation.</p><p>The reason sleep does not fix it is that fatigue was rarely the cause. What you are looking at is some combination of fluid, thinning skin, and lost firmness in the thinnest skin on your body — none of which sleep repairs. Cebeam works on those directly.*</p>"
          }
        },
        "q1": {
          "type": "quote",
          "settings": {
            "quote": "I got eight hours. My boss asked me on a morning call if I’d been up all night.",
            "attribution": "— Sarah K."
          }
        },
        "r2": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "top",
            "heading": "Your face is briefing the room before you say anything",
            "body": "<p>At thirty-two, looking rough reads as working hard. At fifty-four it reads as slowing down. There is a domestic version too, which is worse — you get read as annoyed, checked-out or unapproachable when you are none of those, and you spend energy correcting an impression you never created.</p><p>The shadow under the eye is what the brain reads as fatigue and irritation — it is a hardwired signal. Reduce the shadow and you stop transmitting it. That is the whole return on this: your face stops filing reports you did not authorise.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "quote",
          "settings": {
            "quote": "I look angry even when I’m perfectly happy. My daughter asked why I was mad at her. I wasn’t.",
            "attribution": "— Kevin J."
          }
        },
        "r3": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "top",
            "heading": "Until now there was nothing between a $45 bottle and surgery",
            "body": "<p>This is the real reason so many men do nothing for years. On one side, an eye cream in a black bottle that costs <strong>$45</strong> and does approximately nothing, because a cream cannot get past the skin’s barrier in any meaningful amount. On the other, lower eyelid surgery at <strong>$3,000–$6,000</strong>, general anaesthetic, and <strong>seven to ten days</strong> you cannot turn up to work.</p><p>Cebeam is <strong>[XX]</strong>, takes fifteen seconds, and costs you no days off. It is the middle option that did not exist five years ago.</p>"
          }
        },
        "r4": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "top",
            "heading": "The reason creams fail is delivery, not ingredients",
            "body": "<p>The outer layer of your skin exists to keep things out. Most of any cream you apply never gets through it — some sits on top and ends up on the pillowcase, some evaporates, and a fraction makes it in. [CITATION A — barrier function]</p><p>Cebeam’s cones are made from the actives themselves, dried and compressed, and they start on the other side of that barrier. [CITATION B — microneedle vs topical delivery]</p>"
          }
        },
        "r5": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "top",
            "heading": "It is not a routine, and nobody has to know you use it",
            "body": "<p>Press one patch under each eye before bed, peel them off in the morning. Two nights a week. No cleanser step, no serum, nothing to layer, nothing to rinse.</p><p>It is also invisible. There is no colour in it and nothing sits on your face during the day — it works overnight and it is gone by breakfast. Nobody at work sees a product, and there is nothing on the bathroom shelf that needs explaining.</p>"
          }
        },
